# The greenfield baseline's checksum is recorded in schema_migrations and is meant
# to be immutable and tamper-evident. scripts/schema-equivalence.mjs hashes the
# file's RAW BYTES, so without this pin a Windows checkout (core.autocrlf=true)
# would produce CRLF — and therefore a different digest than Linux/CI for
# byte-identical SQL, making the "immutable" checksum platform-dependent and
# defeating its purpose.
supabase/baseline/*.sql text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for the migration chain: several tests read these files and
# assert on their contents.
supabase/migrations/*.sql text eol=lf

# A third case of the same hazard, found 2026-09-05. An .mjs script that begins
# with a shebang and is imported by a Vitest test fails to parse when the file
# has CRLF, and the suite reports only `SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected
# token` with no line number and no stack.
#
# CI never sees it: CI runs on Linux, where the file keeps LF. So
# scripts/prune-preview-branches.mjs passed the merge gate and then failed on
# every Windows checkout, including the one that created it -- it passed before
# the commit and broke after, because the commit is where the conversion
# happened.
scripts/*.mjs text eol=lf
